00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.229
Without
further ado, I'd like to welcome Ananth and

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:10.560
Mehmet, and tell them to take the stage.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.240
The stage is yours,
gentlemen.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.100
Thank you, Jake.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.750
This is Mehmet Koyuturk.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:16.750
Hello, everyone.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.980
Good morning, good afternoon, or good evening,
depending on where you are.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:29.720
So we are happy to be talking about the effort
on building network databases, which

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:37.220
aims to enable integrated query both biological
networks in real time.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.810
And I will start by motivating what we mean
by that.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.610
What we mean by creating biological networks
and what we

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.730
mean by biological networks, after all.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:54.630
So if we think about the big data of biology,
which people usually call Omics

00:02:54.630 --> 00:03:00.300
in the context of the [INAUDIBLE] of biology,
we have access to data on DNA.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.480
We have access to data on RNA, in
terms of both, the abundance of gene expression

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.800
profiles in a transcription sample.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.320
And also RNA structure,
alternative splicing, and so on.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.980
And then also, at the protein level, we can
measure protein expression, look at protein

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.220
structure.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.500
And then at the
functional level, we can look at protein-protein

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.230
interactions, post-translational modification
of proteins, metabolites,

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.960
and so on and so forth.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.360
And these give rise to [INAUDIBLE] and we
can also measure those things at a very large

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.290
scale, in terms of the phenom types like bio
mass, disease state, clinical outcome, and

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.030
so on and so forth.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:48.170
So in the context of precision medicine, one
important goal is to be able to integrate

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:53.180
the exomic data to make
sense of human diseases, for example.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:04:02.070
And it's important to note that most of this
data can be represented or

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:07.520
naturally comes in the form of networks.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:14.470
And indeed, there are lots of biological databases
out there.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.430
And these networks contain different types
of information, in terms of the interactions

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.820
and associations between
biomolecules.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:29.580
But not just by biomolecules, also different
entities that can be thought of in the context

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.860
of biological
data analysis.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:36.629
So for example, protein-protein interactions
are one form of network data.

00:04:36.629 --> 00:04:40.220
Then you have
information on the physical binding of different

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:41.220
proteins.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.220
And this is usually obtained through high
throughput

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:50.050
experiments, but also protein interactions
can be predicted using computational tools.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:57.009
Then protein DNA interactions gives an idea
about regulatory-- transcriptional regulation,

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.319
and other regulatory
interactions.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:06.110
Small molecule and protein interactions, again,
give an idea about regulation of proteins,

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.550
posttranslational
regulation of proteins.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.569
Enzyme-substrate interactions, put in the
context of metabolic [INAUDIBLE],

00:05:10.569 --> 00:05:14.319
also in the context of post-translational
modification of proteins.

00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:18.240
So these are really at the biological, at
the cellular

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.319
level, interactions that can be represented
as networks.

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:26.990
But going beyond that, also between genes,
we can talk about functional or statistical

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:27.990
interactions.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:34.129
So for example,
if two genes are known to take part in the

00:05:34.129 --> 00:05:38.529
same process, that's a functional interaction
between the genes.

00:05:38.529 --> 00:05:39.560
Or
statistical interactions.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.810
For example, if two genes show up in the same
context, if they are expressed together in

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:53.300
the same context, then this is a statistical
interaction, and it has value to be included

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:57.620
in a natural context in a
network database.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.180
And again, functional interactions between
genomic elements, for example, synthetic lethality.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:06.029
You knock out one
gene in the cell, the cell doesn't die.

00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:07.949
You knock out the other one, the cell doesn't
die.

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:10.360
But when you knock out
both, the cell dies.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:11.889
This is a functional interaction.

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:16.129
Again, all these functional interactions can
be represented as

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:17.189
networks.

00:06:17.189 --> 00:06:24.370
Finally, not just these statistical or physical
interactions, but also associations-- observed

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:27.360
and inferred
associations-- between different biological

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.449
entities can also be represented as networks.

00:06:31.449 --> 00:06:33.610
For example, genotypephenotype
associations.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:41.240
If one gene is identified as being implicated,
or as being associated in a particular

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:46.870
disease, then that is an interaction, or that's
an association, between the gene and the disease.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:52.610
And for example, the OMIM database-- the Online
Mendelian Inheritance in Man database-- contains

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.160
this kind of
information.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.289
And that database can be thought of as a biological
network database.

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:01.809
Similarly, the functional
associations among molecules, the evolutionary

00:07:01.809 --> 00:07:05.910
relationships between genes, can all be represented
in the form

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.289
of networks.

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:17.539
So what you are looking at here, from a paper
that was published in 2011, is a regionalization

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:21.259
of different types of
biological networks and the interactions between

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:22.259
them.

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:25.379
Essentially, different types of biological
network databases

00:07:25.379 --> 00:07:27.919
contain different types of biological network
information.

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:32.430
They do interact with each other, but the
information that

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:38.559
they convey-- the information that they are
focused on-- is different for different databases.

00:07:38.559 --> 00:07:42.289
And people have done
enough with these network databases.

00:07:42.289 --> 00:07:50.460
So for example, by constructing disease gene
networks, researchers are able to transfer

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.090
knowledge on genes
implicated in different diseases to prioritize

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.559
genes to look for another disease.

00:07:57.559 --> 00:08:02.009
And you can do this, really, by
looking at the clinical symmetry between the

00:08:02.009 --> 00:08:04.860
diseases, looking at the functional similarity
between the genes, and

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.360
also looking at the associations between the
diseases.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.680
You can put these all in a network context,
and then use

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.990
that network context to transfer information.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:21.090
So similarly, the concept of phenologs is
based on the representation of evolutionary

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:25.169
relationships between genes
in the form of a network.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:30.069
And then making influences on which genotypes,
in which organisms, match to each

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:31.699
other.

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:39.620
So we have done a lot of work on identifying
tissue-specific networks, or identifying and

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.029
uncovering the
relationship between genes that are mutated

00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:48.589
in cancers versus that are transcriptionally
dysregulated in cancers,

00:08:48.589 --> 00:08:50.100
again, using network context.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.709
But the bottom line is, although these applications
have been successful,

00:08:54.709 --> 00:09:01.730
researchers who do this kind of research--
you don't always need to go to each different

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:02.730
database.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.130
As I said,
different network databases contain different

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.740
types of network information.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.730
So if they will use three types or four types
of different network information, they will

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:18.550
need to go to each database,
download the data from their [INAUDIBLE],

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:28.730
and then they will have to develop algorithms
at home in their own

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:35.740
labs to preprocess, to integrate, and then
to analyze these data so they can make inferences.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:40.070
One question that
we are exploring in this webinar, and also

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:44.820
in our project that was supported by this
big data initiative, is how far

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.910
are we from integrated querying of biological
data?

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.700
That is, can we do these tasks which have
been shown to be

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:57.550
successful again and again-- can we do them
by just going to the web and without needing

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:02.040
to connect to all these
databases?

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:08.170
Querying the databases at once, in real time,
all together for such sophisticated querying

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:09.170
tests.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:15.810
So as you listen to us, really, I will ask
you to think about this question again and

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:16.810
again.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:19.721
Because we will describe
several efforts toward this end.

00:10:19.721 --> 00:10:27.699
And of course, if you are trying to provide
a fast, efficient, and stable querying

00:10:27.699 --> 00:10:33.569
infrastructure for a network, the first thing
that you need to have is a network database.

00:10:33.569 --> 00:10:39.250
And now I will pass the
microphone to Professor Grama, and he will

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:43.220
talk about one of the network databases that--
the database systems

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:55.120
that we are trying to develop.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.770
All right.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:03.380
So the rest of the talk is organized as follows.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.509
I will spend the next few minutes describing
the software

00:11:06.509 --> 00:11:12.970
aspects of this database, all of its features
and the benefits of using this database over

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:16.089
any of the other databases
that you might have encountered.

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:19.780
And then we'll talk about some specific network
algorithms, and then we'll

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:24.030
conclude with some examples of how you might
use an infrastructure like this.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:28.269
So the one condition here is that you have
interaction data that can have millions of

00:11:28.269 --> 00:11:29.540
nodes and edges.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.760
The
networks themselves are continuously updated.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.560
There are many different versions of these
networks.

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:37.350
Networks
are present dynamic systems.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:40.210
So you have to have some integration of molecular
data.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.200
You have to capture network dynamics.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.339
And the key
question that you're posed with is can you

00:11:44.339 --> 00:11:49.709
build a scalable, integrated graph system
that supports versioning, that

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:55.670
supports integrated querying, that supports
analytics, all in place, without requiring

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:59.100
users to install all of these
different tools?

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:04.630
So this effort, for us, led into the development
of what is called Trueno.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.600
This is our hybrid graph
datastore/computational engine.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:14.459
I'll try to impress upon you that this does
have a tremendous set of features for a

00:12:14.459 --> 00:12:15.589
diverse set of applications.

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:18.720
So this is a database that's meant to run
in the cloud.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:19.880
It is distributed.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.190
It's resilient.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.170
And
it's a fast static graph store.

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:29.199
When I say static, it means that once you've
integrated the data into the store, the data

00:12:29.199 --> 00:12:31.449
becomes available for
analytics.

00:12:31.449 --> 00:12:33.980
Static that's here doesn't mean that you can't
update the data.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:37.750
It actually support versioning, it supports
dynamic networks.

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.339
It supports distributed computations.

00:12:40.339 --> 00:12:43.889
It supports online queries, traversals, analytics.

00:12:43.889 --> 00:12:46.629
It scales
to very, very large network databases.

00:12:46.629 --> 00:12:52.480
And when I say large, I mean billions of nodes
and edges is what we have tested these to.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.880
There's literally a
three-mouse-click setup for both cluster and

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.069
single instance installations for these things.

00:13:00.069 --> 00:13:03.660
And it provides a very
powerful, friendly, and intuitive interface

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.649
for network analysis.

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:08.699
It's got a library of tools that already are
built into it.

00:13:08.699 --> 00:13:13.290
And it has hooks for visualization, both to
other tools and a visualization module built

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:14.470
into the database.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.470
All right.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:22.410
So many of you might have used other databases,
Neo4j, OrientDB, Titan.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:25.720
These are commonly used
databases.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.980
And Trueno does a good job of supporting a
number of features that we set out.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.670
You know, first of all,
it's a free open source distributed version.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.950
You can-- at the end of this talk, there'll
be a GitHub repository where

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.769
you can download this from.

00:13:37.769 --> 00:13:38.769
It's easy start.

00:13:38.769 --> 00:13:42.000
Like I said, literally about three mouse clicks
will get you started.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.759
It's got multi-language connectors.

00:13:43.759 --> 00:13:50.060
You can program Trueno queries and applications
in a rich class of languages.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:54.750
It's scalable, in terms of
visualization.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:57.639
Supports GPU-based visualization rendering.

00:13:57.639 --> 00:13:59.750
It's got a rich collection of built-in algorithms
for

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:00.750
analysis.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.490
And finally, it supports dynamic graphs.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:04.560
All right.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:09.140
So it turns out this is a tremendously sophisticated
piece of code, and there's no way any of us

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:10.700
would
write something like this from the ground

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:11.700
up.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.730
So it does leverage a number of existing systems.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.800
At the bottom of it
is a storage layer, which is Cassandra.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:23.000
This is a p-value store that is actually quite
commonly used in such cloudbased

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.040
applications.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.269
On top of it is a visualization layer.

00:14:27.269 --> 00:14:31.579
You have hooks to commonly-used tools such
as Cytoscape and Sigma js.

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:36.089
There is a computational layer that gets your
interface into a very common graph interface

00:14:36.089 --> 00:14:39.269
called GraphX, and
NetworkX, as well.

00:14:39.269 --> 00:14:44.790
And there are language bindings that are supported,
as well.

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:51.430
The architecture of Trueno is shown here.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.000
At the top level, it's got a couple of interfaces.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.509
It's got an interface with
Gremlin.

00:14:55.509 --> 00:15:00.339
This is a language for intuitive and easy
attraction with the graph database.

00:15:00.339 --> 00:15:03.040
It's got a web console, which
means that if you didn't want to actually

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:07.970
ever code anything, there's a nice web-based
interface, as well.

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:09.440
There's a Trueno core.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:15.470
This is essentially what we developed, which
is essentially the core of how graphs are

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:19.199
organized, how graphs are versioned, how you
interface with the front end.

00:15:19.199 --> 00:15:23.930
And of course, underneath Trueno
core, we use everything from Elasticsearch,

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:27.360
which is an indexing technique for fast search.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:32.910
We use Spark, which
is a data processing system used for a distributed

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:33.910
graph processing.

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:35.120
In our case, GraphX, specifically.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:38.319
And of
course, at the bottom of all of this is Cassandra,

00:15:38.319 --> 00:15:42.189
which is a scalable and high availability
common store database.

00:15:42.189 --> 00:15:44.329
All right.

00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:47.050
So the storage schema here is as follows.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:50.290
Each keyspace is a graph.

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:54.230
We have distributed workloaded
storage.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:58.040
You can choose to replicate your data for
increased availability.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:15:59.810
And this is something a user can control.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:05.170
Data is, in fact, sharded, which means that
it is actually broken in little pieces across

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:07.509
different nodes in the cloud.

00:16:07.509 --> 00:16:09.910
And this is to make sure that you have horizontal
scaling.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:12.930
In other words, if you want more throughput,
sharding

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:17.290
gives you more throughput by aligning with
more nodes to get you the data.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.370
It supports multiple properties on
edges and vertices.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:25.750
So for example, if your attractions have properties,
you can put them on edges, [? vertices ?] themselves.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.910
These
maybe expression profiles that you can store

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:28.949
on vertices.

00:16:28.949 --> 00:16:32.740
And finally, analytics results is stored in
graph structure,

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:35.019
as well, for easy post-processing analysis.

00:16:35.019 --> 00:16:40.529
I'm going to go through this a little quickly,
as Mehmet already mentioned this.

00:16:40.529 --> 00:16:45.470
The way people normally work
now, you go to a database, you download your

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:51.040
data, you write analytics in C++ or Java or
Python typically lead to

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.040
a graph library.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.240
You see your results and go from there.

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.470
And what we're looking to do, really, is to
integrate all of

00:16:55.470 --> 00:17:02.269
that into a store so that you can significantly
accelerate and make easy the process of compute,

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:05.410
visualize, and
analyze your results.

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:11.140
This is a typical execution flow for happens
inside Trueno.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:12.250
You get a query.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:16.459
This could be something along the
lines of g.v, which will simply give you all

00:17:16.459 --> 00:17:20.480
the vertices in the graph g, or g.e, which
will give you all the edges in the

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:21.530
graph.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:23.730
The Gremlin server pulls all the data from
the back end.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.520
At this point, you might choose to just visualize
it,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:29.840
or you can look at one of the other key features
here.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.830
All of these things are scheduled.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:35.929
The analytics tasks are scheduled completely
transparent to you on all of the

00:17:35.929 --> 00:17:39.840
cloud resources that are available.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:45.000
And there is a rich set of functionality that
goes with this thing, as well.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.100
The language connectors that you have available,
you can program this thing in Python, Node.js,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:51.590
Java Script,
Java, what have you-- Scala.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:57.350
So it's a very rich set of languages that
are supported, which all get transparently

00:17:57.350 --> 00:18:00.710
executed on this graph database.

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:01.710
The installation is easy.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:06.240
Like I said, a few mouse clicks will install
this thing for you.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:11.970
The data import uses an import
tool to load graphs from JSON-formatted files.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.790
This is not sophisticated.

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:16.159
It can be very easily done.

00:18:16.159 --> 00:18:19.100
The compute
server allows execution of algorithms in a

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:20.850
graph within a few clicks.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:27.350
There are examples in the GitHub page where
we show that very commonly used tools can

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:31.539
be instantiated within a very small number
of clicks, as well.

00:18:31.539 --> 00:18:38.780
It relies on a very fast graph analytic visualization
user interface, which lets you interactively

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:42.440
explore the graph, as
well, in addition to any analytics queries

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.880
that you might choose to write.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:48.050
So this is an example of what a query
might look like.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:50.610
So like I said, feel free to go to the GitHub
repository.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:53.390
There's a complete documentation on this
tool, as well.

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:55.870
We're fairly far along in all of this implementation.

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.020
The core is most implemented.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.990
The web console is mostly
implemented.

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:06.580
What we're working on right now are things
like dynamic graphs and version graphs.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:09.520
But there is a
core functionality that you can easily use

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:14.580
at this point in the version that is available
to you.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:15.580
The performance.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:16.760
We benchmarked this performance against Neo4j.

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:21.289
For those of you who use these things,
Neo4j probably the state of the art.

00:19:21.289 --> 00:19:24.220
It's a fairly expensive piece of software,
too.

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:30.110
So this is about 1.2 to 1.5 times faster on
larger data sets.

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:33.760
The reads and writes, it's 90/10 read-write
mix, similar

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:34.760
to Neo4j.

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:39.290
It's significantly faster than Neo4j on single
write performance.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:43.460
The Compute Engine itself is significantly
faster, for example.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:47.169
Trueno is about twice as fast as Neo4j on
things like

00:19:47.169 --> 00:19:51.740
page rank, fairly simple benchmarks that we
run on these things.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:57.000
So the bottom line is you can choose to use
Neo4j and pay a lot of money, which probably,

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.120
for most of the things that we choose to do,
do not get the same

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:01.230
performance as Trueno.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:05.210
Finally, we show that this is very elastic.

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:08.940
If your throughput requirements go up, this
is very scalable.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:12.481
You can
increase the number of executors and you can

00:20:12.481 --> 00:20:16.000
get fairly good scaling on throughput as you
go through.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:19.370
So this
brings us to the end of the infrastructure

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:20.370
part.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:22.850
I will hand it off to Mehmet now, who can
talk about algorithms, and

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:31.100
we'll come back and talk of use cases in the
applications.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.100
Mehmet.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.600
Thank you, Ananth.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:34.600
OK.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:41.039
So Ananth has already mentioned, for example,
benchmarking Trueno for the PageRank

00:20:41.039 --> 00:20:42.039
algorithm.

00:20:42.039 --> 00:20:46.010
So one question you may have is, why do we
care about PageRank, right, in the context

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.220
of biological
network analysis.

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:50.200
So I will dive into that a little.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:57.510
I will try to motivate a specific type of
query that you can run on these databases,

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.419
what we call network proximate
queries.

00:20:59.419 --> 00:21:04.990
I will try to motivate it, and also, especially
for the computational audience, I will try

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:10.609
to show a few
examples of how interesting computational

00:21:10.609 --> 00:21:11.700
problems can arise from this.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.980
And how we solve from these problems.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:15.860
And then we will go into applications of this.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:23.220
So network proximate queries are essentially
based on the motivation that, say, we had

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.200
a set of nodes in these
networks, and these nodes can be proteins

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:28.950
that are identified in an experiment.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:29.950
Right?

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:34.370
You compared a particle
experimental condition to another, to a control,

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:35.370
whatever.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:39.309
And you identified some proteins as being
significantly

00:21:39.309 --> 00:21:43.270
differentially expressed, significantly differentially
[INAUDIBLE] related.

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:48.299
So what you want to do is you want to ask
the question, what is the functional relationship

00:21:48.299 --> 00:21:49.299
between them?

00:21:49.299 --> 00:21:50.631
Why do
they show up together in an experiment?

00:21:50.631 --> 00:21:56.409
And the second, can you also identify other
nodes, again, in these

00:21:56.409 --> 00:22:04.799
networks that are function-relevant because
I want to be able to functionally notate my

00:22:04.799 --> 00:22:05.799
[INAUDIBLE].

00:22:05.799 --> 00:22:06.799
Right?

00:22:06.799 --> 00:22:10.470
So of course, this is not limited to just
proteins that are identified in an experiment,

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:14.390
but for example, if you have
variants that you identify as being associated

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:16.622
with a disease, again, the same question is
there, right?

00:22:16.622 --> 00:22:20.409
What is the
functional connection between them?

00:22:20.409 --> 00:22:25.809
And then if I already know some variants that
are experimentally related.

00:22:25.809 --> 00:22:32.340
I want to be able to use them to rent
other candidates that I may have.

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:38.230
So these types of questions are endless in
the context of biological exploration.

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:45.890
The nodes, as I said, are not just limited
to molecules or genomic variants.

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:53.070
But any biological entity, like a function,
like a pathway, like a disease, like a species,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:23:00.710
like an organism can be thought of as nodes
in these networks.

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:05.580
So for example, then the network proximity
querying tries to answer this question, of

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.520
what is the functional
connection between them?

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:13.679
Or how can you identify additional nodes that
are functionally related to these query

00:23:13.679 --> 00:23:21.660
nodes is when you map these nodes of interest
to the network, in here, for example, on B,

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:26.350
what you are seeing is
a clustered group of nodes.

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:32.410
If the query nodes are shown in black here,
then we could infer that the white nodes

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:38.080
around them are also probably functionally
related to the experiment that we have, to

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:39.080
the context that we have.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.960
So
they have a good chance of being considered

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:42.960
also.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:48.940
But if, for example, the query nodes are quite
dispersed and on the network, which you would

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:55.630
expect to happen in
random situations, then you will probably

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:59.840
have to do additional searches to see what's
going on here, and why

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:05.220
these proteins or why these nodes are dispersed
across the network.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:08.730
And there could be different explanations
too.

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:15.240
So then the question is, how do we really
quantify the connectedness or the closeness,

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:19.500
or the proximity, between
different nodes in a network?

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:24.580
So to motivate information flow-based algorithms--
and these algorithms have been

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:30.920
used tremendously in the last 5, 10 years
in the context of network analysis.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:32.330
So I have this simple example.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:36.870
Here, what I am showing is three hypothetical
pairs of nodes.

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:41.760
There's s1 and t1, s2 and t2 and s3 and t3.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.940
So when
I ask you the question of, which one which

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:49.940
s-t pair is most likely to be associated with
each other, just based on

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:58.410
this network topology, I believe many will
disqualify [? s-t ?] and t3 because the node

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:03.260
that connects them seems to
be very connected.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:10.720
So they are in direct connection through a4
can be arbitrary and may not mean much.

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:16.039
But for example, if you look at s2 and t2,
there are multiple paths connecting them.

00:25:16.039 --> 00:25:21.870
So the network topology
suggests that actually, they may be talking,

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:25.990
they may be related to each other.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:28.210
And at the [? instance ?] of
dynamic information on biological networks,

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:31.731
really, the models that we have for these
networks are quite, quite

00:25:31.731 --> 00:25:37.840
crude, in the sense that, really, all we know
is whether they bind to each other physically

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:41.299
or they are functionally
associated, and so on so forth.

00:25:41.299 --> 00:25:48.880
A useful measure of network proximity comes
in the form of information flow-based models,

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:52.990
and there are lots of
information flow-based algorithms to assess

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:54.080
network proximity.

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:59.620
So these include, for example, effective
resistance, random walk with restarts, network

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:04.649
propagation, network defusion, defusion state
distance, et cetera.

00:26:04.649 --> 00:26:08.750
And as I said, this has been used a lot in
the context of biology.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:16.080
But almost always by downloading the [? attained
?] bug and then writing, developing your own

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:23.649
code, and then analyzing your data in your
own servers instead of

00:26:23.649 --> 00:26:26.169
running queries on these.

00:26:26.169 --> 00:26:31.590
And of course, this is also [? from the ?] applications
in other domains, like link

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:34.519
prediction, recommender systems, and of course,
we querying.

00:26:34.519 --> 00:26:41.570
And that's where the name Google PageRank--
the name PageRank comes from.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:47.100
So one of the information flow-based algorithms
that is popular is Random Walk with Restarts.

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:50.110
And a
mathematical formulation is out there, but

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:51.860
the general idea is this.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:56.460
So what you do is you have a network and you
have one or more query nodes.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:27:01.679
Let's assume that you have one query node,
for simplicity, but really, usually, in

00:27:01.679 --> 00:27:05.559
the context of the biological applications,
usually you have multiple query nodes.

00:27:05.559 --> 00:27:11.850
Because, again, the query nodes
that you are interested in are usually-- the

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:15.100
purpose of the query is really to understand
the functional connection

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:16.870
between your query nodes.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:20.460
But for simplicity, let's assume that we have
one query node.

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:25.230
And the mathematical model for random walks
is to

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:31.350
simulate a random walk on the network such
that you start from with the node of interest,

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:32.700
from the query node.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:38.000
And then you make a random move to its neighbors,
to one of its neighbors, and you keep going

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:39.250
until a certain
point.

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.289
After that point, you come back.

00:27:41.289 --> 00:27:44.420
You jump back to your origin.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:50.480
And keep doing the random walk, often jumping
back to the query node.

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:58.960
So if you let this go until infinity, if you
let this go forever, this walk go forever,

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:01.859
then the
proximity of each node in the next [? work

00:28:01.859 --> 00:28:06.049
?] to your query node is defined as the probability
of the walk curve

00:28:06.049 --> 00:28:10.279
being in that node at infinity.

00:28:10.279 --> 00:28:15.980
So you can use an algebraic framework to represent
this model.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:19.260
Essentially, you can represent this as a system
of

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:26.649
linear equations where you have a metrics
that presents a network and then essentially

00:28:26.649 --> 00:28:32.850
the proximity vector that
you are looking for is the xq here.

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.790
And rq is your query data.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:40.540
And of course, it is difficult to compute
this directly, so usually, people compute

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:42.019
this iteratively.

00:28:42.019 --> 00:28:46.540
At some point, you
start with a guess, and then you keep updating

00:28:46.540 --> 00:28:47.720
your guess.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:53.530
And eventually, you converge to the stationary
distribution, which gives you the proximity

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:58.860
of each of your nodes to your query node.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:04.419
So this is being used in the context of disease
gene prioritazation, for example.

00:29:04.419 --> 00:29:10.099
Disease gene prioritization is a
problem that-- it came from [? genome-wide

00:29:10.099 --> 00:29:18.490
?] linkage analysis, where you do your linkage
analysis on a limited

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:25.299
set of samples so the part of the genomic
region that you identify as being associated

00:29:25.299 --> 00:29:29.429
with the disease of interest
is usually quite large and it can contain

00:29:29.429 --> 00:29:30.639
hundreds of genes.

00:29:30.639 --> 00:29:35.389
So one question is, how do you range these
genes according to their potential functional

00:29:35.389 --> 00:29:38.269
relevance to the
disease?

00:29:38.269 --> 00:29:45.029
And as early as 2007, algorithms that use
networking information have been developed

00:29:45.029 --> 00:29:46.510
for this purpose.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:52.470
So the idea in network information is, for
example, if you have a set of candidate proteins

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:58.610
that have coding genes
in the region that you identify as being associated

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:02.370
with the disease, then you just go ahead and
identify their

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:04.220
interacting neighbors.

00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:08.980
And then you look at what types of diseases
their neighbors are implied in.

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:16.350
And based on that, you score each of
them, based on the diseases that are associated

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:18.880
with the neighbors of each candidate.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:23.799
And the candidate that is
associated with most similar diseases has

00:30:23.799 --> 00:30:26.590
good potential to be associated with your
disease.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:30.100
So again, these were developed as early as
2007.

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.920
And the first algorithms that have been developed,
first

00:30:32.920 --> 00:30:37.809
methods that have been developed in this context,
really, have treated the networks as a bag

00:30:37.809 --> 00:30:38.809
of interactions.

00:30:38.809 --> 00:30:44.750
Rather than including the network context,
they would just look at immediate neighbors.

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:52.390
But then came algorithms that recognize the
need for looking at network context, and again,

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:56.450
based on the
modulation with multiple paths between different

00:30:56.450 --> 00:31:00.409
nodes in a network might be indicative of
their functional

00:31:00.409 --> 00:31:03.059
association.

00:31:03.059 --> 00:31:07.909
Information flow-based algorithms that use,
for example, random walk with restarts, or

00:31:07.909 --> 00:31:10.269
network
propagation, have been developed for this

00:31:10.269 --> 00:31:11.269
particular problem.

00:31:11.269 --> 00:31:14.710
So you have a set of candidates that are potentially
associated with a disease.

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:20.450
How do you rank them, in terms of their functional--
potential function associated with the disease?

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:26.120
So your query
genes here become the genes that are already

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:31.409
known to be implicated in a disease that is
similar to your disease.

00:31:31.409 --> 00:31:37.030
And then based on that, you rank your candidates
according to the random walk-based proximity

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:42.210
to the query
genes.

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:46.880
But information flow-based algorithms have
an important problem.

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:54.560
And I think I wanted to share this in this
particular webinar because I think it's always

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:58.149
important to be aware of this issue.

00:31:58.149 --> 00:32:10.909
So either for biological or just for
experimental, or even just human-related reasons,

00:32:10.909 --> 00:32:15.350
in biological networks, some of the nodes
will be very well

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:16.350
connected.

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:17.350
OK.

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:25.040
So this might be a protein with a very general
biological function, or this might be a protein

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:28.510
that is well-studied
just because it is interesting.

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:35.730
But some proteins have a high degree, but
many of them have very low degree.

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:42.669
So if you do this decision prioritization,
and if you use an information flow-based algorithm

00:32:42.669 --> 00:32:48.179
to do the ranking, what
you are seeing here is that the algorithms,

00:32:48.179 --> 00:32:53.789
the information flow-based algorithms will
do great if your node is very

00:32:53.789 --> 00:32:54.789
well connected.

00:32:54.789 --> 00:33:00.580
But if your node is not well connected, either
because it is not well studied, or just because

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:04.429
it has a
very specific function, really your algorithm

00:33:04.429 --> 00:33:06.710
will never get studied.

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:09.770
So we developed algorithms to deal with this
issue.

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:15.400
And the algorithms that we developed essentially
assess

00:33:15.400 --> 00:33:21.269
network similarity-- I would say the topological
similarity between two nodes-- not in terms

00:33:21.269 --> 00:33:26.169
of network proximity,
but in terms of the similarity of their network

00:33:26.169 --> 00:33:27.169
profiles.

00:33:27.169 --> 00:33:29.650
So for example, here, if you look at this
hypothetical

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:41.700
example, in this network, although nodes 10
and 12 are not very close to each other, their

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:44.769
proximity profiles-- in
terms of their proximity to other nodes--

00:33:44.769 --> 00:33:45.870
are very similar.

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:50.630
So this may implicate that they may have a
functional relationship.

00:33:50.630 --> 00:33:53.480
But for example, the node that is labeled
12,

00:33:53.480 --> 00:33:58.250
and the node that is labeled three are quite
far from each other.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:03.190
And you can also see this by looking at, again,
their profile, in terms of their proximity

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:04.399
to other nodes.

00:34:04.399 --> 00:34:07.990
So we have shown that this works nicely.

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:13.310
This improves-- this is gene prioritization
candidate.

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:17.159
This is gene
prioritization quite a bit over information

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:19.200
flow-based algorithms.

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:25.990
And it also discovers things that other algorithms
cannot discover because they are always overlooked.

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:31.200
But the catch here is, really, this is even
more expensive because to be able to do that,

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:37.800
you have to generate
these topological profiles, these proximity

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:44.639
profiles, for all nodes, and it's pretty expensive
computationally.

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:48.079
But if
you can see-- really, if you take into account

00:34:48.079 --> 00:34:52.550
this degree bias, then you can develop algorithms
that are not

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:57.030
affected by degree bias that much.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:35:05.530
But to avoid computing all these proximity
profiles-- the network proximity profiles--

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:06.829
what can you do?

00:35:06.829 --> 00:35:10.590
Well, one
thing that you can do is acknowledge the fact

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:11.940
that these are huge networks, right?

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:17.770
There are thousands of
proteins, tens of thousands of biological

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:18.770
functions.

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:22.280
Many organisms, if you connect their networks,
then this will

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:28.760
give you, probably, networks with hundreds
of thousands of nodes, maybe millions of edges

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:31.010
and so on, so forth.

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:33.060
So these are huge.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:41.310
And probably, the information for each node
in these networks lies around its close neighborhood.

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:44.400
So instead of
really computing these proximity vectors by

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:50.310
[? all, ?] what you can do is you can just
try to identify the K, and K

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:51.849
being a magic number here.

00:35:51.849 --> 00:35:56.720
It can be 1,500-- I don't know-- depending
on your application, depending on the

00:35:56.720 --> 00:36:02.440
user's needs who is querying these networks,
or 10.

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:10.330
We can define the problem as identifying the
top K, most proximate nodes to a query node,

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:14.230
or to a set of query
nodes.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:18.710
In the data mining community, people have
studied these problems.

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:22.910
And the idea usually that is employed
is either use the structural properties of

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:28.950
the networks to avoid doing the entire computation
and just quickly walk

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:31.590
around the query nodes.

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:36.860
And by using the structure, they can stop
immediately after you are sure that you found

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:38.290
the K closest nodes.

00:36:38.290 --> 00:36:41.610
Or
use the convergence properties of the iterative

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:44.320
computation and our understanding of [? molecular
?] linear

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:50.119
algebra and [INAUDIBLE] modeling of the residual
data that you have with each iteration.

00:36:50.119 --> 00:36:51.760
[INAUDIBLE] to make a
quick stop.

00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:58.420
Instead of really going on to converence,
stop when you are sure that you have found

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:03.370
the top K entries
in the proximity vector that you never want

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:05.490
to compute.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:10.870
So what we did around this line was used Chebyshev
acceleration for this purpose.

00:37:10.870 --> 00:37:16.490
So the idea here is,
essentially, in the context of that iterative

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:23.009
computation, what you can do is, instead of
using the iterative [? steps--

00:37:23.009 --> 00:37:28.720
?] the iterative that you use for the proximity
vector itself-- you can use it in your combination.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:32.350
And if you're using it
in your combination with Chebyshev polynomials,

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:36.520
you can show that linear combination will
converge to your

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:42.230
desired proximity vector more quickly than
the iterite itself.

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:50.850
So using this nice result, you can also bound
the error that you make at each iteration,

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:55.290
essentially, how much you
deviate from the actual solution.

00:37:55.290 --> 00:38:02.230
And use that again to decide when to stop
when you identify the top K closest

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:03.230
neighbors.

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:13.569
And also, although I said, you need a linear
combination of the formula iterates, you can

00:38:13.569 --> 00:38:19.340
really still do it
without storing all the iterates, without

00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:21.240
increasing the storage at all.

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:28.480
So you can think of the idea here as also
remembering-- in the context of the random

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:32.900
walk on the network, what
you are doing is you are remembering where

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:34.280
you pass through.

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:36.740
Instead of just looking at the future, you
also look

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:38.820
at the past and remember.

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:41.630
You look back and remember where you are coming
from.

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:47.710
So that is what you are
doing by also looking at the previous iterates.

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:50.470
And again, this can be done quite efficiently.

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:59.869
And when you test this on networks with millions
of nodes, what you see is over standard [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:59.869 --> 00:39:02.660
iteration
this type of acceleration will start much

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:03.660
more quickly.

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:08.550
So here, what you are looking at is, on the
x-axis, the

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:11.970
number of iterations for each dataset.

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:17.050
And the y-axis is how much you are away from
the actual solution.

00:39:17.050 --> 00:39:21.260
So the
red curve converges much more fast compared

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:22.670
to blue curve.

00:39:22.670 --> 00:39:32.150
So the consequence of this is, for example,
depending on how many nodes you are trying

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:41.290
to identify, either 4 or 8
or 32-- of course, if you are trying to identify

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:45.089
a larger number of nodes that are closer to
your query, then you will

00:39:45.089 --> 00:39:46.859
have to iterate more.

00:39:46.859 --> 00:39:51.230
So that's why the number of iterations goes
up, sK goes up.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:55.890
But as you can see, the red
curve, which is Chebyshev's acceleration,

00:39:55.890 --> 00:40:00.761
does it still in much less number of iterations,
as compared to other

00:40:00.761 --> 00:40:09.559
methods that use either the structure of the
network or just the basic properties of the

00:40:09.559 --> 00:40:12.770
iterative computation--
basic [? medical ?] properties of the iterative

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.770
computation.

00:40:13.770 --> 00:40:14.819
So this is quite fast.

00:40:14.819 --> 00:40:20.530
And then also, there is one parameter in these
competitions always, the alpha perimeter.

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:29.010
I think I didn't talk about
that when I first introduced the concept of

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:30.470
random walk with restarts.

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:37.540
So the parameter alpha here controls how
much, how local you want to make your search.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:44.090
So with a larger alpha, then you keep going
back to your query node again and again.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:49.090
Whereas for smaller alpha,
you don't keep going back that much, so the

00:40:49.090 --> 00:40:53.470
network context becomes more important, and
the choice of alpha--

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:58.120
usually most algorithms are robust to this
choice of alpha, but it can still be important,

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:03.210
in terms of also the
computational efficiency and the accuracy

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:04.210
of your results.

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:09.190
So this particular algorithm that I am talking
about, the Chebyshev acceleration.

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:16.339
Also depending on whether your
search is local or more global, the improvements

00:41:16.339 --> 00:41:21.270
in computational performance will depend on
whether you

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.200
search is local or global.

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:27.270
So again, for smaller alpha, your search is
more global.

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.230
For larger alpha, your search is
more local.

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:35.820
So for more global search, this type of acceleration
will be more useful.

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:40.859
Because for local search, the
problem is much easier.

00:41:40.859 --> 00:41:47.940
So then, motivated by this, we have also developed
algorithms to make these even faster.

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:51.109
Because still, if you
think about integrating different types of

00:41:51.109 --> 00:41:57.910
biological networks into this huge network,
even after Chebyshev [? sK ?]

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:03.130
acceleration, the time that is required to
process these queries may not be real times.

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:07.530
Still, we may have to wait
for minutes to process a query.

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:13.780
So thinking about how we can further accelerate
this, one idea that we have just

00:42:13.780 --> 00:42:23.260
implemented and verified that it works is
to index some of the solution.

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:30.520
So essentially, since this is a linear system,
what you can do is you can always take the

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:33.890
inverse of the matrix that
is involved, store that matrix, and then when

00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:37.510
a query comes, you can just multiply that
inverse with [? two-query ?]

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:38.700
vector, and you are done.

00:42:38.700 --> 00:42:40.520
You have the solution.

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:44.890
So it is just one matrix make the [INAUDIBLE]
application that you need to do during query

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:45.890
processing.

00:42:45.890 --> 00:42:48.700
But when
you do that, I mean, the problem with that

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:52.970
is for such a large network, the inverse will
be dense-- computing

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:53.980
inverse will be very difficult.

00:42:53.980 --> 00:42:56.050
It will require the aid of memory.

00:42:56.050 --> 00:42:59.030
And storing that inverse will take a lot of
storage.

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:01.660
So
it's not really feasible to index it.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:05.109
But the key observation is that these networks
are highly sparse.

00:43:05.109 --> 00:43:08.720
As I said before, in these networks, most
of the

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.069
nodes will be very loosely connected.

00:43:11.069 --> 00:43:14.210
They will have just one, two, three neighbors.

00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:23.600
So what you can do is you can use graph partitioning
to divide this network into one dense chunk.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:28.910
And then these
very little parts that are loosely connected

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:32.390
within each-- maybe that are a little densely
connected within each

00:43:32.390 --> 00:43:34.940
other, but loosely connected to the network.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:39.250
And the matrix that corresponds to network,
if you re-order your

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:46.819
matrix according to the graph partitioning,
what you will get is a very large, but very

00:43:46.819 --> 00:43:50.320
sparse, network that is shown
as M11 here.

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:55.190
And a very dense but small network that is
shown as [? M22 ?] here, and an M12 and M21

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:59.780
are the
connections between these parts of the network.

00:43:59.780 --> 00:44:04.520
But now what you can do is you can take the
inverse of M11 and index that.

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:10.450
And you can solve the part of the
linear system that corresponds to M22 using

00:44:10.450 --> 00:44:14.370
the iterative computation that I described
just a few minutes ago.

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:16.910
And
then you can integrate these solutions at

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:20.680
query time, and this turns out to be really,
really fast.

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:26.720
So comparing these million [INAUDIBLE] networks
like that, what we were able to see is if

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:34.390
you try to index the
entire inverse-- that's the purple bar that

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:39.109
you are looking at there-- essentially, it
will only work on small networks.

00:44:39.109 --> 00:44:47.730
We are not even able to get results on larger
networks because, again, it's not scalable.

00:44:47.730 --> 00:44:55.900
But if you index in such a way that you divide
the network into two parts, where one part

00:44:55.900 --> 00:45:02.310
is large and sparse, the
other part is small and dense, then you will

00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:03.970
increase your index size slightly.

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:12.349
So compared to, for example, the red
method that it uses just the iterative computation--

00:45:12.349 --> 00:45:15.520
so if you are just using iterative computation,
all you need to

00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:18.420
store is the matrix itself, or the network
itself.

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:25.650
So you are [? relying ?] a bit on that storage,
but the improvements in query time that you

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:30.900
are getting is two orders
of magnitude faster than the standard iterative

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:31.900
computation.

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:36.860
So this is very promising in terms of really
making

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:41.340
real time integrated querying of biological
networks possible.

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:46.869
So once these are integrated into Trueno,
essentially, we will be able to deliver these

00:45:46.869 --> 00:45:52.950
type of queries in real time within seconds,
probably.

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:58.119
So the first application that I will talk
about-- and I will just briefly go through

00:45:58.119 --> 00:46:03.890
this, and then pass it to Ananth so that
he can talk about even more exciting applications.

00:46:03.890 --> 00:46:09.599
So one thing that we did, in utilizing these
algorithms, in terms

00:46:09.599 --> 00:46:18.040
of discovering biological networks, was applying
this to predicting drug response in cancer

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:19.040
cell lines.

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:20.720
So for that
purpose, we downloaded data.

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:23.819
Again, as you can see, we downloaded the data.

00:46:23.819 --> 00:46:27.590
So we are not still quite there, in terms
of querying the data

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:29.060
directly from the databases.

00:46:29.060 --> 00:46:30.589
But we are getting there.

00:46:30.589 --> 00:46:37.170
So this is a really encouraging, nice experiment,
showing

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:40.040
that we can get there.

00:46:40.040 --> 00:46:46.650
So we downloaded two types of cell line datasets
with drug treatment with different cancer

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:47.650
drugs.

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:49.330
So one of them
is the GDSC.

00:46:49.330 --> 00:46:53.770
One of them is downloaded from GDSC, the Genomic
of Drug Sensitivity in Cancer.

00:46:53.770 --> 00:46:57.630
It's about 700
cell lines, and screened for under 40 drugs.

00:46:57.630 --> 00:47:03.710
And the Cancer Cell Line Encyclopedia contains
about 500 cell lines

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:06.859
that are screened for 24 drugs.

00:47:06.859 --> 00:47:12.730
And if you have ever [INAUDIBLE] this problem,
this is a quite-- you will know that,

00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:16.339
from a machine learning perspective, this
is a quite difficult problem.

00:47:16.339 --> 00:47:21.339
And actually, there are lots of competitions
trying to make this possible.

00:47:21.339 --> 00:47:28.540
So then what we did is we developed a heterogeneous
network model representing all the information

00:47:28.540 --> 00:47:30.680
that is in
these databases.

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:34.609
So the orange nodes show the drugs, the blue
nodes show the cell lines.

00:47:34.609 --> 00:47:40.301
The green nodes show
the genes, and an edge between a cell line

00:47:40.301 --> 00:47:43.630
and a gene shows that the gene is mutated
in the cell line.

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:47.940
A purple
edge between a cell line and a drug shows

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:54.680
that that cell line is sensitive to the drug,
and the pink edge shows that

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:57.859
a cell line is resistant to the drug.

00:47:57.859 --> 00:48:02.270
And then the edges, the yellow edges between
the genes show that those genes

00:48:02.270 --> 00:48:07.880
are coding for proteins that are functionally
or physically interacting.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:14.820
So using this heterogeneous network, then
can we predict drug response for cell lines?

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:21.280
So the framework for that
is-- so we separate the networks based on

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:28.810
these pink edges versus the dark blue edges
to create sensitivity

00:48:28.810 --> 00:48:33.430
networks that are representing sensitivity
to drug, and networks that are representing

00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:35.270
resistance to drug.

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:44.190
Then we do these network proximity queries
by representing the drug as the query node,

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:49.430
and then we try to
identify a profile-- the proximity profile

00:48:49.430 --> 00:48:50.430
for the drug.

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:55.490
So the proximity profile, if you will, will
be essentially the genes

00:48:55.490 --> 00:49:08.930
that are mutated or that are close to the
mutated genes in the cell lines that are either

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:12.809
sensitive to the drug, as in
the sensitive network, or resistant to the

00:49:12.809 --> 00:49:14.839
drug, as in the resistance network.

00:49:14.839 --> 00:49:19.710
Then we look at the similarity between these
profiles.

00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:22.500
And by comparing the similarity between the
resistance

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:30.410
profiles versus the sensitivity profile, we
predict whether a given cell line is responsive

00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:31.829
to a drug.

00:49:31.829 --> 00:49:36.670
And when we test
this on the GDSC dataset using leave-one-out

00:49:36.670 --> 00:49:40.260
cross-validation-- the idea in leave-one-out
cross-validation is you

00:49:40.260 --> 00:49:46.630
are removing one edge in the network at a
time, one edge being an edge between a drug

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:48.030
and a cell line.

00:49:48.030 --> 00:49:51.330
You are
removing that and you are trying to see whether

00:49:51.330 --> 00:49:52.849
you can predict that edge.

00:49:52.849 --> 00:49:57.290
So the x-axis here represents the sensitivity
score.

00:49:57.290 --> 00:50:00.819
The y-axis represents the resistance score.

00:50:00.819 --> 00:50:03.869
And the color code
represents the [? IC50 ?] [INAUDIBLE], which

00:50:03.869 --> 00:50:07.819
represents the response to the drug.

00:50:07.819 --> 00:50:10.200
And it is not used in the
predictions.

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:18.220
So as you can see, the predicted [INAUDIBLE]
can cluster the drug cell line pairs based

00:50:18.220 --> 00:50:21.710
on the [? IC50 ?]
[INAUDIBLE] quite well.

00:50:21.710 --> 00:50:28.369
So then we looked at the [INAUDIBLE] patterns
of this by using the area under the curve

00:50:28.369 --> 00:50:30.130
as a performance criterion.

00:50:30.130 --> 00:50:35.559
And we have seen that this was quite robust
to the parameter that was involved in the

00:50:35.559 --> 00:50:36.690
network proximity computations.

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:43.369
And actually, [INAUDIBLE] that parameter became
one, which means that the network was not

00:50:43.369 --> 00:50:45.040
important at all at
this point.

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:47.620
Only the mutation information was used.

00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:50.410
The performance went down, showing that the
network

00:50:50.410 --> 00:50:54.990
information is quite variable.

00:50:54.990 --> 00:51:01.230
And interestingly also, when we do the training
on one database, and then when we do predictions

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:04.140
on the other
database, with no cross- [INAUDIBLE] between

00:51:04.140 --> 00:51:05.140
databases.

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:10.339
And Also, this is completely honest validation
where

00:51:10.339 --> 00:51:16.110
really, you are using completely independent
databases with very different properties.

00:51:16.110 --> 00:51:22.690
Still we were able to predict
drug response for the cell lines and CCLE.

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:27.440
Much, much better [INAUDIBLE] available area
under the curve of

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:31.380
86%, which is pretty good for drug response
prediction.

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:36.530
Again, as I said, it's pretty difficult to
make predictions using molecular data.

00:51:36.530 --> 00:51:41.540
So I will stop at this point, and I will
pass it on to Ananth so that he can talk about

00:51:41.540 --> 00:51:46.670
two more exciting applications because we
have 10 minutes left.

00:51:46.670 --> 00:51:48.230
Ananth?

00:51:48.230 --> 00:51:49.790
Ananth.

00:51:49.790 --> 00:51:54.460
I'm good, Mehmet.

00:51:54.460 --> 00:51:59.900
I need the screen.

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:05.770
You need the screen.

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:07.730
OK.

00:52:07.730 --> 00:52:13.609
Sorry about this.

00:52:13.609 --> 00:52:22.400
Microsoft has a way of starting you at the
start, and I couldn't leave my screen at full

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:26.059
screen.

00:52:26.059 --> 00:52:28.060
So
I'll talk about two more examples.

00:52:28.060 --> 00:52:31.510
The first one gives you a sense of how you
construct tissue-specific

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:32.760
interactomes.

00:52:32.760 --> 00:52:34.329
And so the problem is as follows.

00:52:34.329 --> 00:52:38.150
I'll quickly summarize this because I do want
to leave some time for questions.

00:52:38.150 --> 00:52:43.010
So the setting is actually quite commonly
encountered, where you have a set of genes,

00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:47.260
and in order to [? actually
?] express, the greens are definitely expressed,

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:50.200
and the reds are not.

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:53.790
And you're trying to find, in this context,
what are the pathways that are significantly

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:56.230
implicated.

00:52:56.230 --> 00:52:59.020
So here, A represents the original.

00:52:59.020 --> 00:53:06.869
B, C, and D all represent different possible
alternatives for what might be the

00:53:06.869 --> 00:53:09.619
implicated networks in this particular context.

00:53:09.619 --> 00:53:12.630
So if you want to solve a problem like this,
you can actually set up a

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:14.980
very simple optimization problem.

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:18.040
What you do is you want to make sure that
you diffuse the pattern of

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:20.650
dysregulation through the network.

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:24.670
But you want to make sure that the entire
network doesn't get implicated in this

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:26.099
regulation.

00:53:26.099 --> 00:53:31.410
So you have some kind of regularizer, which
is an L1 regularizer with suitable sum to

00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:32.780
1 constraints
here.

00:53:32.780 --> 00:53:36.410
And if you solve this, you can actually define
something called activity scores.

00:53:36.410 --> 00:53:40.060
So you've taken your dysregulation,
you've defined activity scores.

00:53:40.060 --> 00:53:44.010
You can use these activity scores with fairly
conventional computer science

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:46.589
techniques, things like Prize-Collecting Steiner
Trees.

00:53:46.589 --> 00:53:49.840
And now you can start to actually find disease-specific
networks.

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:52.000
I'll give you two examples of Parkinson's
and

00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:53.390
Alzheimer's.

00:53:53.390 --> 00:53:55.859
And so what we do is we start from transcriptomic
data.

00:53:55.859 --> 00:53:57.960
We find the dysregulated genes.

00:53:57.960 --> 00:54:00.520
We
integrate networks with these dysregulated

00:54:00.520 --> 00:54:01.520
genes.

00:54:01.520 --> 00:54:03.849
And then we use this infrastructure that I
just mentioned to pull

00:54:03.849 --> 00:54:09.660
out networks that are very specifically implicated
in a specific phenotypic context.

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:14.530
So these are simple examples, but you can
actually do a much broader study.

00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:19.290
In this particular case, we pulled
out context-specific networks from a number

00:54:19.290 --> 00:54:21.270
of diseases.

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:24.119
And here we show the P values of the compactness
of

00:54:24.119 --> 00:54:25.640
the disease-implicated networks.

00:54:25.640 --> 00:54:30.180
So once again, here, low P value means that
you're much more likely to find

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:34.049
disease-implicated genes within these sub
networks that you pulled out.

00:54:34.049 --> 00:54:39.030
And in each of these cases, you will notice
that the method that we have, which is [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:39.030 --> 00:54:43.690
85, gives you
really good P-values.

00:54:43.690 --> 00:54:48.430
The aggregate P-value is 3.7 times 10 to bar
minus 19, which tells you that these networks

00:54:48.430 --> 00:54:53.109
that we're pulling out are very, very specific
to the disease contexts for which we pulled

00:54:53.109 --> 00:54:54.450
out the transcriptomic
data.

00:54:54.450 --> 00:55:00.670
So it's a really clean example of how you
can build context-specific networks.

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:03.670
I'll just quickly summarize one more
very recent paper.

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:07.230
I think this will appear in Nature Communications
within the next two weeks.

00:55:07.230 --> 00:55:10.910
And this applies
similar network techniques to single cell

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:12.180
data.

00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:15.990
And here, the idea is you have a large number
of single cell profiles.

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:21.680
How do you find a network context, or network
underlying these single cell transcriptomic

00:55:21.680 --> 00:55:23.000
profiles?

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:26.430
So here's an example of something that we
pulled off.

00:55:26.430 --> 00:55:28.809
These were about 80,000 cells, so think of
these as 80,000

00:55:28.809 --> 00:55:29.809
So here's an example of something that we
pulled off.

00:55:29.809 --> 00:55:31.030
These were about 80,000 cells, so think of
these as 80,000

00:55:31.030 --> 00:55:35.990
transcriptomic profiles derived from, I think,
about tens of patients-- about 20 patients

00:55:35.990 --> 00:55:36.990
here.

00:55:36.990 --> 00:55:39.930
And these were
melanoma patients.

00:55:39.930 --> 00:55:44.910
So in this particular case, after all of the
post-processing that we do based on what is

00:55:44.910 --> 00:55:48.520
called a principal Convex-
Hull analysis, you can recast all of those

00:55:48.520 --> 00:55:50.640
cells in a lower dimensional space.

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:53.221
And you see these really neat clusters
that start to pop out.

00:55:53.221 --> 00:55:55.900
So these are, for example, the NK CD80 cells.

00:55:55.900 --> 00:55:58.010
These are the CD4 T-cells.

00:55:58.010 --> 00:56:01.180
But really, the
cluster you're interested in is the tumor

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:03.079
cluster here.

00:56:03.079 --> 00:56:06.849
And you find some very interesting sub-clusters
within this cluster, as well.

00:56:06.849 --> 00:56:13.020
So the most conventional differentiation
that you see is based on the expression of

00:56:13.020 --> 00:56:15.770
this metF gene, metF high or metF low.

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:17.910
This is what is clinical practice
right now.

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:20.760
But we, in fact, show that there are other
clusters, as well.

00:56:20.760 --> 00:56:24.170
We pull out the networks associated with these
clusters.

00:56:24.170 --> 00:56:26.420
So we take one of those archetypes.

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:30.460
We look at all the transcription factors and
the dysregulated genes.

00:56:30.460 --> 00:56:35.210
We can find all the drug targets and we can
show very different survivability for all

00:56:35.210 --> 00:56:37.660
of these genes, as well.

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:42.970
So this is another example of how you can
use all

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:48.880
the way from transcriptomic data down to networks
to really, really target precision medicine

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:52.880
down to a cell, not
just an individual.

00:56:52.880 --> 00:56:58.809
So to conclude, network models are very effective
ways of representing, analyzing disparate

00:56:58.809 --> 00:57:00.170
data.

00:57:00.170 --> 00:57:03.080
Network
topology matters, and there are massive computational

00:57:03.080 --> 00:57:04.080
challenges.

00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:08.380
I think we're at a point where we have made
significant progress.

00:57:08.380 --> 00:57:10.420
We've made progress in the software infrastructure.

00:57:10.420 --> 00:57:13.930
We've made progress in the
methodological space.

00:57:13.930 --> 00:57:18.369
And we've been able to show some really compelling
applications, as well.

00:57:18.369 --> 00:57:22.350
So a number of wonderful students and colleagues
worked on this.

00:57:22.350 --> 00:57:27.039
Our acknowledgments to the National Institute
of Health, the National Cancer Institute,

00:57:27.039 --> 00:57:30.119
and specifically, to the BD2K Program.

00:57:30.119 --> 00:57:33.170
Thank you very much.

00:57:33.170 --> 00:57:34.170
Thank you, gentlemen.

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:35.170
This was really, really interesting.

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:37.280
Looks like a very interesting tool.

00:57:37.280 --> 00:57:41.900
I want to ask any of
our audience members to please text in any

00:57:41.900 --> 00:57:43.670
questions that you might have.

00:57:43.670 --> 00:57:47.990
To kind of kick things off on a couple
of questions in the time that we have left,

00:57:47.990 --> 00:57:52.160
I was curious-- I didn't quite pick it up,
and perhaps you touched on this

00:57:52.160 --> 00:57:58.970
and I missed it, but are the networks-- you
have adjacency matrices, which I presume are

00:57:58.970 --> 00:58:00.220
just like 1's and 0's.

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:02.799
Can
you have weighted networks, as well?

00:58:02.799 --> 00:58:07.660
Can those networks be directed graphs, or
are those not possible, or add

00:58:07.660 --> 00:58:09.150
extra levels of complication?

00:58:09.150 --> 00:58:13.990
[INTERPOSING VOICES]
But I can answer, as well.

00:58:13.990 --> 00:58:17.099
Would you like to go ahead, Mehmet?

00:58:17.099 --> 00:58:19.069
It's fine for me.

00:58:19.069 --> 00:58:20.069
OK.

00:58:20.069 --> 00:58:24.480
Let me try to answer and then you can add.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:31.760
So yes, essentially, [INAUDIBLE] are an important
part of these networks because, for example,

00:58:31.760 --> 00:58:36.760
for interactions between proteins, it's either
your confidence on the

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:42.710
existence of that interaction, or the affinity
between the proteins usually has to be taken

00:58:42.710 --> 00:58:43.710
into account.

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:47.369
And there
have been several studies that have showed

00:58:47.369 --> 00:58:52.990
that if you take into account such confidence,
or affinity scores, then

00:58:52.990 --> 00:58:57.880
usually, your utilization of the network will
be more effective, in terms of uncovering

00:58:57.880 --> 00:58:58.880
true biology.

00:58:58.880 --> 00:59:00.589
So those really
matter.

00:59:00.589 --> 00:59:05.780
In terms of directed networks, yes, most of
these edges have to be directed because, for

00:59:05.780 --> 00:59:08.420
example, for a [? regular
?] interaction, right?

00:59:08.420 --> 00:59:13.050
If a gene acts on the other, if the gene regulates
the other, then you need to be able to

00:59:13.050 --> 00:59:16.079
encode that information.

00:59:16.079 --> 00:59:19.500
So in the database, you can easily account
for this.

00:59:19.500 --> 00:59:23.891
But in terms of the analytics, in terms
of the algorithms that are involved, or in

00:59:23.891 --> 00:59:28.549
terms of the query that you define on this,
you may lose that information.

00:59:28.549 --> 00:59:35.910
So the proximity queries that I have talked
about today can be made to take into account

00:59:35.910 --> 00:59:41.310
or ignore the direction,
depending on whether you want to include that

00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:42.789
information or not.

00:59:42.789 --> 00:59:47.311
If you want to include direction, then you
may

00:59:47.311 --> 00:59:53.039
need to be more specific on how you want to
incorporate it because the way that I define

00:59:53.039 --> 00:59:59.140
network proximity, it was
essentially, how close you are in the network.

00:59:59.140 --> 01:00:05.970
So when the direction comes into play, it
may be more difficult to semantically define

01:00:05.970 --> 01:00:06.970
that.

01:00:06.970 --> 01:00:09.810
So Ananth, if you would
like to add anything to this.

01:00:09.810 --> 01:00:10.810
Sure.

01:00:10.810 --> 01:00:15.569
And by way of the software infrastructure,
the edge and the node schemas are very rich.

01:00:15.569 --> 01:00:18.920
You can store
arbitrary information on both the edges and

01:00:18.920 --> 01:00:19.920
the nodes.

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:21.819
For example, on the edges, you may want to
have a time

01:00:21.819 --> 01:00:22.819
series.

01:00:22.819 --> 01:00:26.099
These are interactions that change the time,
so you can actually put a complete time series

01:00:26.099 --> 01:00:28.981
on the edges,
in addition to directionality and all the

01:00:28.981 --> 01:00:31.410
other attributes you might want.

01:00:31.410 --> 01:00:34.440
The node schema itself is arbitrarily rich.

01:00:34.440 --> 01:00:37.670
You can put time series or any other information
on the node schema, as

01:00:37.670 --> 01:00:38.670
well.

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:44.559
So the software admits all of these-- the
database completely supports all of these.

01:00:44.559 --> 01:00:45.650
I'm glad you brought that up.

01:00:45.650 --> 01:00:50.500
I was going to ask about how whether or not
you could do things to the edges and

01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:53.359
make queries on the edges and what have you.

01:00:53.359 --> 01:00:54.359
Absolutely.

01:00:54.359 --> 01:00:55.359
Yeah, yeah.

01:00:55.359 --> 01:00:56.359
That's interesting.

01:00:56.359 --> 01:01:01.070
One final question before we have to go is
this looks like a really fantastic tool for

01:01:01.070 --> 01:01:02.940
training and
education.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:09.870
And I was curious if it's possible, and perhaps
it is, to synthetically break a network to

01:01:09.870 --> 01:01:14.510
be able to load
one in and maybe, with a pair of digital scissors,

01:01:14.510 --> 01:01:18.089
go and clip some connections or turn some
nodes off.

01:01:18.089 --> 01:01:21.940
And then
watch what happens when you've done that.

01:01:21.940 --> 01:01:25.099
And that can happen, you know, as a training
exercise.

01:01:25.099 --> 01:01:28.869
Is that
something that you have pursued?

01:01:28.869 --> 01:01:31.410
So the basic database does not have this functionality.

01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:35.349
But it's absolutely-- I mean, if this is a
kernel or an analytics

01:01:35.349 --> 01:01:39.700
kernel that people believe is important, it
would not take us very long to code this into

01:01:39.700 --> 01:01:40.700
the database.

01:01:40.700 --> 01:01:42.850
Of course,
you could code this yourself, but even if

01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:44.859
you wanted us to code it, it wouldn't take
very long.

01:01:44.859 --> 01:01:47.440
It would just be a
bunch of graphics queries to remove a few

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:52.119
nodes or remove a few edges to separate the
vertex.

01:01:52.119 --> 01:01:54.280
The [? vertex ?]
the graphic could do.

01:01:54.280 --> 01:01:58.550
You could do this through the web API.

01:01:58.550 --> 01:02:00.420
That's easily done, at this point.

01:02:00.420 --> 01:02:02.930
But beyond that, you would have to code
what it is that you're trying to do with the

01:02:02.930 --> 01:02:05.270
two sub networks that you get.

01:02:05.270 --> 01:02:06.299
But your point is very well-taken.

01:02:06.299 --> 01:02:09.599
I think
such experiments really need to be set up.

01:02:09.599 --> 01:02:11.670
The core infrastructure is there.

01:02:11.670 --> 01:02:15.170
We need to set up these experiments
beyond that.

01:02:15.170 --> 01:02:17.349
Well, that's great.

01:02:17.349 --> 01:02:20.130
Well, it looks like we are out of time.

01:02:20.130 --> 01:02:24.599
I want to think Ananth and Mehmet for their
presentation.

01:02:24.599 --> 01:02:29.170
This looks like a very valuable tool, and
one I'm going to actually go and take a look

01:02:29.170 --> 01:02:31.119
at once I get back to the US.

01:02:31.119 --> 01:02:32.819
I'm abroad right now.

01:02:32.819 --> 01:02:34.480
But this looks like a great tool.

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:36.160
I hope others will do that, as well.

01:02:36.160 --> 01:02:40.569
I want to thank you guys again, and I want
